# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

Successfully implemented Vercel Speed Insights for the Banana FC Tournament project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Added Dependencies
- **Modified**: `package.json`
  - Added `@vercel/speed-insights@^1.3.1` to dependencies

### 2. Integrated SpeedInsights Component
- **Modified**: `src/main.jsx`
  - Imported `SpeedInsights` component from `@vercel/speed-insights/react`
  - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component alongside the existing `<Analytics />` component within the ErrorBoundary wrapper

## Implementation Details

This is a React + Vite project that already had Vercel Analytics configured. Following the Vercel Speed Insights guide for React applications, I:

1. Installed the `@vercel/speed-insights` package using npm
2. Imported the `SpeedInsights` component in the main entry point (`src/main.jsx`)
3. Placed the component at the root level alongside the existing Analytics component for optimal tracking

The implementation follows React best practices and is consistent with the existing code structure. The SpeedInsights component will automatically track Core Web Vitals and performance metrics once the app is deployed to Vercel.

## Verification

- ✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
- ✅ No new linting errors introduced in modified files
- ✅ Dependencies updated in package.json
- ✅ Lock file (package-lock.json) updated

## Next Steps

Once deployed to Vercel with Speed Insights enabled in the dashboard:
1. The tracking script will be available at `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js`
2. Performance metrics will start appearing in the Vercel dashboard under the Speed Insights tab
3. Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB) will be collected and displayed
